A phylogenetic model assigns a probability distribution to observed leaf states on a tree using hidden internal states and stochastic transition matrices along edges. A phylogenetic invariant is a polynomial vanishing on that model. Flattening groups tensor indices into blocks to produce a matrix or lower-order tensor; rank constraints can then give polynomial minors. The exact constraints depend on the mutation model and state cardinality. A stochastic parameterization restricts parameters to nonnegative normalized probabilities; a complex parameterization allows complex coordinates and is often studied through its Zariski closure.
